think Read the road-ahead trio (REMEMBER, the 1024-round itinerary, the six-season double-seat) and the rye-first crypto-parity decision wave, then the ML-KEM/ML-DSA pivot doc. The living edge was the THIRD post-quantum rung (mlkem_sample.rye, GREEN). The pivot's rung ladder names rung 4 — mlkem_keygen.rye, K-PKE + ML-KEM.KeyGen — as the FIRST rung with a doubled oracle. Highest-Lindy (the whole crypto library serves every module that signs or agrees keys), crux-first (keygen is the decisive composition that opens encaps/decaps and the ML-DSA ladder), gate-free (public test vectors only). Clear next lap.
think Studied the composition surface before authoring: mlkem_ring (ntt, inv_ntt, multiply_ntts, Poly{cs:[256]u32}, all canonical residues), mlkem_encode (byte_encode(f,d)->Encoded, max_d=12), mlkem_sample (sample_ntt(rho,b0,b1), sample_cbd(bytes,eta)), sha3 (hash512 for G, hash256 for H), shake (shake256 for PRF). Then read Zig's own vendored std/crypto/ml_kem.zig to fix conventions exactly: innerKeyFromSeed does G=SHA3-512(d || k_byte) for ML-KEM (the appended rank byte), A_hat via uniform(rho, j, i) untransposed so A[i][j]=SampleNTT(rho,j,i) and t[i]=sum_j A[i][j].s[j], noise=SHAKE256(sigma || nonce) then CBD, s over nonces 0..k-1 and e over k..2k-1, both eta1 (eta2 is for encryption). Confirmed the honest parity boundary the pivot flagged: Zig's th uses dotHat().toMont() to cancel Montgomery and normalize() to [0,q), and Poly.toBytes is exactly ByteEncode12 — so despite Zig computing in Montgomery domain internally, it agrees with our FIPS standard-domain arithmetic at the encoded byte boundary. PublicKey.toBytes = th.toBytes ++ rho = ek; SecretKey.toBytes = sh ++ ek ++ H(ek) ++ z = dk. Exact FIPS layout.
think Authored crypto/mlkem_keygen.rye: the keygen function composes only GREEN rungs and calls no std.crypto; the selftest alone invokes Zig std.crypto.kem.ml_kem as the parity oracle (same shape as the classical arc's Monocypher-diff witnesses). Both computed in one binary and diffed byte-for-byte keeps the primitive pure while the doubled oracle stays decisive. Parameterized keygen by comptime k and eta1 with three pub-callable parameter sets; fixed max_k=4 buffers.
obs GREEN on the first build+run: our pure-Rye ek and dk match Zig's generateDeterministic(seed)->toBytes byte-for-byte at ML-KEM-512 (k=2 eta1=3), ML-KEM-768 (k=3 eta1=2), and ML-KEM-1024 (k=4 eta1=2) over 8 deterministic seeds each, a trivial all-zero key refused before the diff. This is the arc's first EXTERNAL oracle — it validates the ring rung's coefficient ordering, the matrix (j,i) indexing, the PRF nonce sequence, and the ek/dk serialization all at once against an independent implementation. The self-consistency the ring/encode/sample rungs stood on is now confirmed by a second stranger.
obs TAME style bans clean (mlkem_keygen.rye and its witness carry contract asserts on every function, bounded loops citing k/n/eta, canonical-residue invariants); the new files do not appear in the zero-assert ratchet. crypto/README.md synced same round with the keygen row. REMEMBER git nib refreshed to the keygen living edge in the same work commit.
